\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{mathtools}
\usepackage{amssymb}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\begin{document}

Find $k$ such that the polynomial $x^4 + kx^3 + x^2 + 4kx + 16$ has exactly one real root:
\begin{align*}
\text{Discriminant of the polynomial: }
f^{(4)}(x) &= 12 (k^2 - 4), \\
\text{Roots of the polynomial: }
f(x) &= (x + 2)(x - r)(x - r^2), \\
\text{Discriminant } = f''(r)^2&- 4f'(r)^3 + 16f(r)^2 \\
&= f'(r)^2 \left[ 12 (k^2-4) - 4f(r)\right] \\
\text{For exacly one real root, the discriminant } &= 0 \\
\implies 12 (k^2-4) - 4f(r) &= 0.
\end{align*}

Hence, we should find all values of $k$ such that
\boxed{
12 (k^2-4) - 4f(r) = 0.
}
Since the fourth-degree polynomial $f(x)$ has real roots, the Discriminant Root Theorem guarantees that the roots $r$ and $r^2$ are real, too. Numerically, we can approximate the roots $r$ and $r^2$: $\boxed{r \approx -2, r^2 \approx 4}$. Hence, using $r^2$ in the equation, we get:
\begin{align*}
12 (k^2-4) - 4f(r) = 12 (k^2-4) - 4(r^2 + 2r + 4) = 0, \\
\text{which simplifies to: } 24(k^2 - 4) - 4(2r + 8) = 0, \\
\text{which further simplifies to: } \boxed{k^2 - 2r - 1 = 0}.
\end{align*}

Now, we substitute the approximate value $-2$ for $r$ back, and solve for $k$:
\begin{align*}
k^2 + 2k + 1 &= 0, \\
(k+1)^2 &= -1, \\
\text{which has exactly one real root, namely: } &<simplest_exact_value> = \sqrt{-1}. \\
\text{**The Question was about }k,\text{ but recall }k = i\sqrt{-1} = i. \text{ We replace } k = i. \text{ Therefore, the final answer: } \\
\boxed{k = i}.
\end{align*}
```

