\left(\dfrac{x}{3}\right)^3-3x^2+81x-737& \stackrel{!}{=} 0
\end{align*}

Step 2: Our goal is to find the root(s) of the equation $f(x)=\left(\dfrac{x}{3}\right)^3-3x^2+81x-737$.

Step 3: Apply synthetic division using $(3,81,-737)$ as the divisor parameters and $x$ as the variable to generate the synthetic division table:

|   |  3  | 81 | -737 |
|---|----|----|-----|
| $x$   | **1** | **27**   | **-127**  |
| $3x$  |  3  | 219  |  -381  |
| $\frac{x}{3}$ | $\boxed{\textbf{1}}$   | $\boxed{\textbf{9}}$    | $\boxed{\textbf{-41}}$  |

Step 4: The remainder is $-41$, and the term $3x-41$ divides the polynomial $f(x)$.

Step 5: Factor out $3x-41$ from the polynomial:
\begin{align*}
f(x)=\left(\dfrac{x}{3}-41\right)(x^2+9x-7)
\end{align*}

Step 6: Find the roots of the quadratic equation $q(x)=x^2+9x-7$, using the quadratic formula:
\begin{align*}
x_{1,2}=&\dfrac{-b\pm\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a} \\
=&\dfrac{-9\pm\sqrt{9^2-4(1)(-7)}}{2(1)} \\
=&\boxed{\textbf{4}}, \boxed{\textbf{-\frac{11}{2}}}
\end{align*}

Step 7: Combine the factors in the quadratic equation to get the complete solution:
\begin{align*}
f(x)=\left(\dfrac{x}{3}-41\right)\left(x-\dfrac{11}{2}\right)(x-4)
\end{align*}

