\boxed{(2^3)^{(2^3)} = 2^N \quad \Rightarrow \quad (2^3)^{2^3} = 2^N \quad \Rightarrow \quad (8)^{8} = 2^N}

Now, we compare the right side (which is a power of $2$) to the left side
(which is a power of $8$):

\boxed{8^3 = 2^N \quad \Rightarrow \quad N = 3 \times \log_2{8}}

Note that, $\boxed{\log_2 8 = 3}$ so,

\boxed{N = 3 \times 3 = \boxed{9}}

Thus, $\boxed{N = 9}$.

