\text{ Midpoint }(M) &= \left(\frac{x_A+x_B}{2},\frac{y_A+y_B}{2}\right) \\
                   &= \left(\frac{2+10}{2},\frac{5+5}{2}\right) \\
                   &= (6,5).
\end{align*}

The radius of the circle is half the distance between points A and B:

\begin{align*}
\text{Radius }(r) &= \frac{\sqrt{(x_A-x_B)^2+(y_A-y_B)^2}}{2} \\
                &= \frac{\sqrt{(2-10)^2+(5-5)^2}}{2} \\
                &= \frac{\sqrt{84}}{2} = \frac{\sqrt{2\cdot42}}{\boxed{2}}.
\end{align*}

Now, let's find the area of the circle:

\begin{align*}
\text{Area }(A) &= \pi r^2 \\
               &= \pi\left(\frac{\sqrt{2\cdot42}}{\boxed{2}}\right)^2 \\
               &= \pi\left(\frac{\sqrt{2\cdot42}}{\boxed{2}}\right)\left(\frac{\sqrt{2\cdot42}}{\boxed{2}}\right) \\
               &= \boxed{\dfrac{\pi\cdot42}{\boxed{4}} \text{ square units }}.
\end{align*}

To find the number of square units in the area of the circle, we should divide the area by the area of one square unit:

\begin{align*}
\text{Number of squares } (N) &= \dfrac{\text{Area }(A)}{\text{Area of one square }} \\
                                &= \dfrac{\text{Area }(A)}{1 \text{ square unit}} \\
                                &= \dfrac{\pi\cdot42}{\boxed{4 \text{ square units}}}.
\end{align*}

To simplify this expression:

\begin{align*}
N &=  \dfrac{\pi\cdot42}{\boxed{4 \text{ square units}}} \\
