Recall that two integers $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$, denoted $a \equiv b \pmod{n}$, if $n$ divides $a-b$, i.e., if $a$ and $b$ leave the same remainder when divided by $n$. The inverse of $a$ in the multiplicative group is an integer $b$ such that $a \times b \equiv 1 \pmod{n}$.

