\begin{align*}
\log_b(13 \cdot 15) &= \log_b(243) \tag{definition of logarithm} \\
\log_b(13) + \log_b(15) &= \log_b(243) \tag{properties of logarithms}
\end{align*}

Assuming we have the common logarithm function ($b=10$) and the natural logarithm function ($b=e$), we can look up their values:

- For base 10:

\begin{align*}
\log_{10}(13) + \log_{10}(15) &= \log_{10}(243) \tag{common logarithms} \\
\left( \log_{10}(13)\right) \text{ (approx.) } + \left( \log_{10}(15)\right) \text{ (approx.) } &= \log_{10}(243) \tag{substituting the approximate values} \\
1.07918 + 1.17609 &= 2.27496 \text{ (approx.)} \tag{rounding to three decimal places} \\
2.25527 &= 2.27496 - 0.01969 \tag{subtracting the approximate values} \\
\boxed{b \approx 10^{2.25527}} \tag{base 10 approximation}
\end{align*}

- For base $e$:

\begin{align*}
\log_e(13) + \log_e(15) &= \log_e(243) \tag{natural logarithms} \\
\left( \ln(13)\right) \text{ } + \left( \ln(15)\right) &= \ln(243) \tag{natural logarithms in terms of ln} \\
6.91474 + 3.68870 &= \ln(243) \tag{substituting natural logarithm values} \\
10.60344 &= \ln(243) \tag{subtraction} \\
\boxed{e^{10.60344}} \tag{base e approximation}
\end{align*}

We can see that both approximations are indeed very close to the base $b$ that makes $13\cdot15=243$ valid. However, note that both the common and natural logarithm functions are continuous at $13$, so we cannot say that $13$ is _exactly_ an integer power of 10 or of $e$. In practice, we can round the answers to a fixed number of decimal places according to the desired accuracy. In this case, we rounded to three decimal places: $b\approx10^{2.25527}$ or $e^{10.60344}$.

