The formulas for the interior angles of a regular pentagon and hexagon are:
  - Interior angles of a regular pentagon = $\frac{5}{2} \cdot 180 \degree = 108 \degree$
  - Interior angles of a regular hexagon = $360 \degree$

Since the pentagon and hexagon share a side (and thus their vertices are collinear), the measure of angle $BAC$ is the difference between the sum of the other interior angles of the hexagon and the sum of the interior angles of the pentagon:

\boxed{ BAC = (360 \degree - 5 \cdot 108 \degree) }

To simplify, first compute the sum of the interior angles of the pentagon and the hexagon:

\boxed{ \begin{aligned}
   5 \cdot 108 \degree + 360 \degree &= 4720 \degree  \\
   6 \cdot 108 \degree - 6 \cdot 108 \degree &= 0 \degree
  \end{aligned} }

Then subtract the sum of the interior angles of the pentagon from the sum of the interior angles of the hexagon to find the measure of angle $BAC$:

\boxed{
    \begin{aligned}
       (4720 \degree - 0 \degree) &= 4720 \degree  \\
       \frac{4720 \degree}{ 1 } &= \boxed{ 4720 \degree }   % <-- output this line
    \end{aligned}
}

