To solve this matrix problem, we need to find the standard matrix representation of a projection onto the $y$-axis.
The standard basis vectors for the Cartesian plane are $\hat{i}$ and $\hat{j}$.
We can find the standard matrix $[P]$ for the projection $P$ onto the $y$-axis by finding the orthogonal projection of $\hat{i}$ onto the $y$-axis, that is, the component of $\hat{i}$ that lies along the $y$-axis, and express it as a multiple of $\hat{j}$.
Since the projection of $\hat{i}$ onto the $y$-axis lies in the $y$-axis, its $x$-component is always zero; thus, we can write the orthogonal projection of $\hat{i}$ onto the $y$-axis as $\hat{i} = \begin{bmatrix}0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} = -1\hat{j}$, because scaling by $-1$ preserves the magnitude of the vector while reversing its direction.
As a result, the standard matrix representation of the projection onto the $y$-axis is:
\begin{align*}
[P] &= \left[ \hat{i} \cdot \hat{j}, \ \hat{j} \cdot \hat{j} \right] \\
    &= \begin{bmatrix} -1 & 0 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} + \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix} \\
    &= \boxed{ \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix} }
\end{align*}

