Let's first find a basis for the y-axis. Since the y-axis consists of all scalar multiples of the vector $\langle0, 1\rangle$, this vector is a basis for the y- axis.

Now let's create the matrix that projects vectors onto the y-axis. To do this, we find the coordinates of the projection of the standard basis onto the y-axis.

For the standard basis vector $\langle1, 0\rangle$, the projection is the zero vector $(\underbrace{0*\cos(\theta)}_{\text{x component}} + \underbrace{0*\sin(\theta)}_{\text{y component}}, \underbrace{0*\cos(\theta)}_{\text{x component}} + \underbrace{1*\sin(\theta)}_{\text{y component}}) = \langle0,1\rangle$.

For the standard basis vector $\langle0, 1\rangle$, the projection is already the same vector $\langle0,1\rangle$.

So the matrix that projects vectors onto the y-axis is:

