Here is the step by step solution:

1. A matrix that corresponds to projecting onto the $y$-axis is a matrix that only has zeros in its first column and the other elements are the same as the original matrix.

2. Let's say the original matrix is $A$, of size $n \times n$. The matrix that projects onto the $y$-axis can be represented as:

   \begin{align*}
   P &= \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 & \dots & 0 & 0 \\
        0 & 0 & \dots & 0 & 0 \\
        \vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots & \vdots \\
        0 & 0 & \dots & 0 & 0 \\
        0 & 0 & \dots & 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix}_n \\
      &= \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ \vdots \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}_n \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 & \dots & 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix} = 0 \cdot A + A \cdot 0 \,\,\,  (projection matrices are idempotent) \\
      &= A \cdot 0^T \,\,\,\,  (substituting the transpose since the projection matrix is symmetric)
   \end{align*}

3. So the matrix that projects onto the $y$-axis can be represented as $P = A \cdot 0^T$

4. Note that if the original matrix was already 2-D artificial units may appear as the dimensionality of the matrix cannot be reduced. In this case, the above steps still apply, and there will not be any artificial units in the answer.

\boxed{A \cdot 0^T}

When you use the final expression in an actual calculation, don't forget to multiply $A$ by the transpose of a row vector (or a column vector) of zeros.

For example: If $A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 & 3 \\ 4 & 5 & 6 \end{bmatrix}$, then the matrix $P$ that projects onto the $y$-axis for this $2 \times 3$ matrix is:

\begin{align*}
P &= \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 4 & 5 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
\end{align*}

