```
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{amssymb}
\usepackage{mathtools}

\begin{document}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item How many numbers are there between $3$ and $201$? Let the count start at $0$.
        \begin{align*}
            \text{Count} & = \sum_{\substack{n=0 \\ 3 \leq n+3 \leq 201}}^{201} 1 \\
            & = \sum_{n=0}^{67} 1 \\
            & = 68
        \end{align*}

    \item In part (a), counting immediately gave us the $51^\mathrm{st}$ number. We would like to find $n$. Conversely, this means taking the difference between the expected number of count (i.e., $n$) and the seen count ($51$):
        \[
            n = 51 + 68 - 1 = \boxed{125}
        \]
\end{enumerate}

\end{document}
```
Here is how you can solve the problem using LaTeX:

The document is set up using AMSMath and amssymb for mathematical notation, mathtools for certain environment and alignment of equations, and article for writing the document.

1. Determine the number of integers between $3$ and $201$. We let the count start at $0$.

```
\begin{align*}
\text{Count} & = \sum_{\substack{n=0 \\ 3 \leq n+3 \leq 201}}^{201} 1 \\
&\quad\text{Sum from $0$ to the number of steps between $3$ and $201$.} \\
& = \sum_{n=0}^{67} 1 \\
&\quad\text{Since $3 \leq n+3 \leq 201$ implies $n \geq 0$ and $n \leq 67$}\\
& = 68
\end{align*}
```

2. To find $n$, we need to take the difference between the expected number of count (i.e., \(n\)) and the observed count (\(51\)).

```
n &= \text{expected count} - \text{observed count} \\
   &= 51 + 68 - 1 \\
   &= \boxed{125}
```

Et voila, the answer is \boxed{125}.

