This problem requires understanding what it means for a function to be even and odd, and then finding the conditions for a function to be both even and odd.

An even function is a function such that for all x in the domain, f(-x) = f(x).
An odd function is a function such that for all x in the domain, f(-x) = -f(x).

A function that is both even and odd is known as an exceptional function or a singular function, because it must satisfy both the even function and odd function criteria. The only function that is both even and odd is the zero function (f(x) = 0 for all x).

Here is how we can solve the problem in LaTeX:

We start by defining the zero function:

\begin{align*}
f(x) &= 0
\end{align*}

The domain of the function is the set of all real numbers.

Next, we check if the function is even:

\begin{align*}
f(-x) &= 0 & \\
f(x) &= f(-x) & \text{So $f(x)$ is even.}
\end{align*}

Next, we check if the function is odd:

\begin{align*}
f(-x) &= -0 & \\
f(x) &= -f(-x) & \text{However, this is not true, so $f(x)$ is not odd.}
\end{align*}

Since the function is not odd, it cannot be both even and odd. Therefore, the number of functions that are both even and odd is zero, or more concisely:

\boxed{0}

The units are not relevant in this case, as there are no physical units involved. If we were to add units for clarity, it would be: \boxed{0 \text{ functions}}

