First, we need to find equivalent angles: $\cos n^\circ = \cos 568^\circ \implies n^\circ = 568^\circ - 360^\circ k,$ $k = 0,1,2,\ldots.$

The cosine function has a period of $360^\circ,$ so we only need to consider the principal range $0^\circ \le n^\circ \le 360^\circ.$
Let's look at the piecewise function of $\cos x$ in this range:

\begin{align*}
\cos x &= \begin{cases}
\cos(x + 360^\circ k) & x \notin [0^\circ, 360^\circ), k = 0,1,2,\ldots \\
\cos x & x \in [0^\circ, 360^\circ)
\end{cases}
\end{align*}

Since $568^\circ - 360^\circ k \notin [0^\circ, 360^\circ),$ $k = 1,$ as we go along $k = 0,1,2,\ldots$ $n^\circ = 568^\circ - 360^\circ = \boxed{208^\circ}.$

