To solve this equation, we can first rewrite it as a single trigonometric identity:
 \begin{align}
 3\sin\theta-\sin 3\theta&=4\sin^3\theta\\
 \Rightarrow&\ \cos\theta(\sin3\theta-3\sin\theta)=-4\sin^3\theta\\
 \Rightarrow&\ \sin3\theta-3\sin\theta=-4\sin^3\theta \cos\theta
 \end{align}

We need to find an angle $\theta$ that satisfies the equation above.
Since $\sin\theta$ is a periodic function with period $\pi$, we can consider $\theta  \in\ [0,\pi/2]$. It turns out that the tangent functions can help us in this situation:
 \begin{align}
 \tan3\theta&=\dfrac{\sin 3\theta }{\cos 3\theta }\\
 \Rightarrow&\ \tan\theta&=\dfrac{1-9\sin^2\theta }{3-3\cos^2\theta }
 \end{align}

We are now looking for a fixed point of this function for $\theta \in[0,\pi/2]$.
Plug in different values of $\theta$ into the right-hand side of the equation above until we get a satisfying $\theta$. The first few values of $\theta$ and their corresponding tangents are
 \begin{align}
 \theta &\approx 0:& \tan\theta &= 0\\
 \theta &\approx \frac{\pi}{6}:& \tan\theta &= 0.5774\\
 \theta &\approx \frac{\pi}{4}:& \tan\theta &= 1\\
 \theta &\approx \frac{\pi}{3}:& \tan\theta &= 1.7321
 \end{align}

By keeping increasing $\theta$, we find the smallest positive solution for our problem:
 \begin{align}
 \theta&=0.5236\text{ radians = }30.98^{\circ}\text{, approximately } \boxed{30.98^{\circ}}
 \end{align}

