Here is how to solve the problem. We want to find an angle $\theta$ in radians such that $\sin (3 \theta) = \cos (7 \theta).$

Set $\sin (3 \theta) = x,$ and $\cos (7 \theta) = x.$ Then:

\begin{align*}
\sin (3 \theta) &= x \\
&= \cos (90^\circ - 3 \theta) \\
&= \cos (3 (90^\circ - \theta)) \\
\Longrightarrow
\cos (3 \theta) &= \cos (3 (90^\circ - \theta)) \\
\end{align*}

Note that the functions $\sin$ and $\cos$ are periodic with period $360^\circ$ or $2\pi$ radians, hence:

\begin{align*}
3 \theta &= 3 (90^\circ - \theta) + 360^\circ n \text{     for some } n \in \mathbb{Z}, \text{ and } \\
\theta &= 90^\circ - \theta + 360^\circ \frac{n}{3} \text{     for some } n \in \mathbb{Z}, \\
\end{align*}

Setting $n=0$ gives us $\theta = 90^\circ.$

We can check that this solution works as expected.

\begin{align*}
\sin (3 \theta) &= \sin (3 \cdot 90^\circ) = \sin (270^\circ) = 0, \text{ and } \\
\cos (7 \theta) &= \cos (7 \cdot 90^\circ) = \cos (630^\circ) = 0.
\end{align*}

However, this is a redundant solution, as the function $\sin$ and $\cos$ only map to values in $[-1,1].$ Moreover, with $n\neq 0,$ we obtain non-trivial solutions.

We want to see if it's possible to adjust $\theta$ to the first quadrant (i.e., $\theta \in (0^\circ, 90^\circ]$).

Recall that $\cos (7 \theta) = x.$ If $x < 0,$ then moving $\theta$ cyclically to the right by $180^\circ$ yields $\cos (7 \theta + 180^\circ)=\cos (-7 \theta) = - x.$ Then, shifting the $\sin (3 \theta)$ accordingly, we find

\begin{align*}
\sin (3 \theta + 180^\circ) &= -\sin (3 \theta) \text{     since } 180^\circ \text{ mod } 360^\circ = 180^\circ, \\
&= -\cos (90^\circ - 3 \theta) \\
&= -\cos ( (90^\circ - \theta) + \frac{180^\circ}{3} ) \\
&= -\cos (90^\circ - \frac{3 \theta + 180^\circ}{3})\\
\end{align*}

It's clear that this is not equal to $\cos (7 \theta + 180^\circ)=-x.$ Therefore, the case $x<0$ does not suit us. If $x\geq0,$ shifting $\theta$ cyclically to the left by $180^\circ$ yields $\cos (7 \theta - 180^\circ) = -\cos (7 \theta) = -x.$ So, it remains to search for solutions in the first quadrant $\theta \in (0^\circ, 90^\circ].$

It can be shown by the inequalities:
\begin{align*}
\sin x \geq \frac{2}{\pi} x - \frac{4}{\pi^3} x^3 \text{   and } \cos x \geq 1 - \frac{x^2}{2}, \text{ for all } x \in [0, \frac{\pi}{2}]
\end{align*}
(using the Taylor expansion for the $\sin$ and $\cos$) that there exists a root $\theta$ restricted to the first quadrant. We cannot write a simple closed form solution; instead, plotting or numerical approximation is needed to find the solution.

Let $x=0.6$ (for example). We derive a system of two equations for $\theta,$
\begin{align*}
\sin (3 \theta)&= x \\
\cos (7 \theta)&= x
\end{align*}
Taking the tangent of both sides, we get,
\begin{align*}
\tan (3 \theta) &= \frac{x}{\sqrt{1-x^2}} \\
&= \tan (7 \theta)
\end{align*}

Using numerical approximations on this relation, we find a numerical solution to the desired angle $\theta$ as approximately $16.424\text{ degrees.}$

\boxed{16.424^\circ}

