Let \(s\) be the side length of the base cube, and \(V_s\) be its volume. We have:
\[V_s = s^3 = 8 \text{ cubic centimeters \quad ( since $8 \times 8 \times 8 = 512 = 2^9 = 8^3$ ) }\end{equation}
The larger cube has a volume of \(V_s + 19\) cubic centimeters. Since the volume of a cube is equal to the side length cubed, we have:
\begin{equation}
(s+1)^3 = V_s + 19 = 8^3 + 19 = 601 \text{ cubic centimeters} \quad {\Rightarrow} \quad s+1 = \sqrt[3]{601}
\end{equation}
Therefore, the length of one edge of the new cube is:
\begin{equation}
s = \boxed{ \sqrt[3]{600} } \text{ centimeters}
\end{equation}

